<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hakor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 380 BCE</span> · Also known as Achoris</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hakor, called Achoris by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, was the strongest king of the Twenty-ninth Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, reigning for about thirteen years in the 390s and 380s BCE. He allied with Evagoras of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, hired Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> under the Athenian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chabrias">Chabrias</a> and repelled a Persian attack around 385 to 383 BCE. He built widely in Egypt, though many of his works were later reinscribed by his successors.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hakor.jpg" alt="Hakor" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Flinders Petrie (1853-1942) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_Achoris_Petrie_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
